diff --git a/doc/Event-naming-spec-0.0.0.md b/doc/Event-naming-spec-0.0.0.md
index 28b9f49..17ed62f 100644
--- a/doc/Event-naming-spec-0.0.0.md
+++ b/doc/Event-naming-spec-0.0.0.md
@@ -101,6 +101,8 @@ These events are meant to be used by system components.
 #### Notifications
 
 - *battery-caution*: The event used when the battery is nearing exhaustion (below 40%, for example).
+- *battery-charging*: The event used while the battery is being charged. The
+  feedback lasts until the event is ended when charging stops.
 - *battery-full*: The event used when the battery is fully loaded up.
 - *device-added*: The event used when a device has become available to the desktop, i.e. due to USB plugging.
 - *key-pressed*: The event used when a key on e.g. an on screen keyboard is pressed.
diff --git a/doc/feedback-themes.rst b/doc/feedback-themes.rst
index a796045..1707282 100644
--- a/doc/feedback-themes.rst
+++ b/doc/feedback-themes.rst
@@ -46,6 +46,25 @@ You can use the following feedback types to build a theme:
 - `VibraPattern`: Specifies a pattern of rumbles using the haptic motor.
 - `Led`: Blinks a LED in a periodic pattern.
 
+Every feedback type takes an optional `priority` (0 to 255, default 0).
+A haptic motor plays one effect and an LED shows one pattern at a time.
+When feedbacks of different events compete for a device the one with
+the higher priority wins:
+
+- An LED shows the pattern with the highest priority (the newest one
+  among equal priorities); when it ends the next one is shown again.
+- A vibration ends a running vibration of a lower priority; otherwise it
+  is dropped. Every event takes priority over the haptic interface
+  (``org.sigxcpu.Feedback.Haptic``), which is refused while an event
+  vibrates.
+- A sound is dropped while a sound of a higher priority plays.
+
+Applications that are not listed in the ``allow-important`` setting use
+at most priority 128. The default theme uses 192 for alerts (incoming
+calls, alarms, cell broadcasts), 128 for new messages and
+notifications, 120 for the reminders of missed ones and 64 for the
+charging indicator.
+
 Sound feedback
 ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
 
@@ -97,7 +116,8 @@ The `Led` feedback type uses two properties to specify how an LED blinks:
   two-digit hexadecimal values between ``00`` and ``FF``, specifying
   the values of the red, green, and blue components, respectively. For
   example, ``#00FFFF`` represents cyan.
-- `frequency`: The LED blinking frequency, in millihertz (mHz).
+- `frequency`: The LED blinking frequency, in millihertz (mHz). ``0``
+  gives a constant light.
 
 `Led` feedback is usually used only in the `silent` profile section of
 a theme.
